{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ae419a19-9800-48af-96a5-6768a7c10d34","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Refraction changes a light ray's direction when it crosses between materials. Measure every angle from the normal, not the surface.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Snell's law: refraction"},{"op":"diagram","id":"boundary","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.08,0.5],[0.92,0.5]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.1],[0.5,0.9]],"stroke":"dashed","label":"normal"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.18,0.18],"content":"air: n = 1.00"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.2,0.82],"content":"glass: n = 1.50"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ae419a19-9800-48af-96a5-6768a7c10d34","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Entering glass from air, the ray bends toward the normal. Its angle in glass is smaller than its angle in air.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"incoming","canvas_id":"boundary","from":[0.16,0.16],"to":[0.5,0.5],"stroke":"solid"},{"op":"arrow","id":"outgoing","canvas_id":"boundary","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.682,0.84],"stroke":"solid"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"angleone","canvas_id":"boundary","center":[0.5,0.5],"radius":0.17,"start_deg":225,"end_deg":270,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ₁"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"angletwo","canvas_id":"boundary","center":[0.5,0.5],"radius":0.2,"start_deg":62,"end_deg":90,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ₂"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ae419a19-9800-48af-96a5-6768a7c10d34","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Snell's law balances refractive index times sine of the angle. A larger refractive index means a smaller angle from the normal.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"snell","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"n_1\\sin\\theta_1=n_2\\sin\\theta_2"},{"op":"equation","id":"indices","anchor":{"el":"snell","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"n_2>n_1\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\theta_2<\\theta_1"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If light travels from glass into air, does the transmitted ray bend toward or away from the normal?","expected_gist":"Away from the normal, provided a transmitted ray exists."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ae419a19-9800-48af-96a5-6768a7c10d34","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For a forty-five-degree incident angle, substitute the two indices. The refracted angle is about twenty-eight degrees, consistent with bending toward the normal.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"substitute","anchor":{"el":"indices","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\sin\\theta_2=\\frac{1.00}{1.50}\\sin45^\\circ\\approx0.471"},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"substitute","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"\\theta_2\\approx28.1^\\circ<45^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":null}}
